winewayland: never DEMOTE a subsurface when its anchor cannot be computed

Premiere's plugin and extension windows sank behind the Program Monitor the moment they were
dragged or docked, recovering only when the user clicked away from the app and back. This is
Bug A of the 11.10-64 regression and the reason owned-subsurface-captioned -- the fix that makes
Premiere's panels dockable at all -- had to be defaulted OFF.

CAUSE. wayland_surface_reconfigure()'s subsurface branch re-anchors the surface on EVERY
reconfigure, and a drag or a dock IS a reconfigure:

    if (toplevel_data->client_surface && <sibling check>)
        wl_subsurface_place_above(surface->wl_subsurface, client_surface->wl_surface);
    else
        wl_subsurface_place_above(surface->wl_subsurface, toplevel_surface->wl_surface);

place_above(X) means "immediately above X", so the fallback places the window immediately above
the PARENT -- the BOTTOM of the sibling stack, beneath the Program Monitor's own surface.

MEASURED, and this is the part that was assumed for hours and turned out to be the whole answer:
**Premiere's main toplevel has no client surface at all.**

    NT-EV restack toplevel=0x10122 zlist=64 applied=2
    NT-SKIP hwnd=0x10122 reason=no-client-surface (d=0x555586312410 cs=(nil) ...)

The preferred branch can therefore NEVER be taken for this app, and every reconfigure demoted the
window. It also silently undid the win32 z-order mirror, which is why NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER
measured as "provably runs, changes nothing" -- the mirror placed the plugin on top and the next
reconfigure put it back at the bottom.

FIX: NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE=1 -- an anchor we cannot compute is simply not applied. The
existing order stands and the z-order mirror owns stacking, which is the Windows model (the app
owns z-order). A subsurface is already above its parent by default at creation, so nothing needs
the fallback in order to become visible.

RESULT, operator-confirmed: panels dock without sinking, and a VST3 plugin window dragged over
the Program Monitor stays on top.

REQUIRED SET for dockable panels (all four, they do not work apart):
    NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED=1   promote captioned owned windows
    NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE=1           or the window cannot be MOVED at all
    NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER=1                 the mirror, which now actually sticks
    NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE=1         this patch
Defaults are unchanged pending a full working session.

⛔ TWO WRONG FIXES ON THE WAY HERE, both from assuming instead of measuring:
  * "place once per role assignment" -- froze the FIRST anchor, which is the bottom one, so it
    made the sink permanent instead of intermittent.
  * "re-run the mirror at role assignment" -- the mirror was never the problem; it was correct
    and being undone.
The measurement that settled it was one probe printing WHY each window was skipped.

⚠️ STILL OPEN: undocking a panel does nothing at all. Unrelated to stacking.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -958,6 +958,30 @@
     surface->ever_had_content = TRUE;
 }
 
+/***********************************************************************
+ *          neutron_no_demote   (NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE=1 enables)
+ *
+ * The reconfigure path anchors a subsurface with wl_subsurface.place_above(X), which means
+ * "immediately above X". It prefers the toplevel's client (video) surface and falls back to the
+ * toplevel's own wl_surface -- and place_above(parent) is the BOTTOM of the sibling stack.
+ *
+ * MEASURED 2026-08-27: Premiere's main toplevel has NO client surface at all
+ * (NT-SKIP hwnd=0x10122 reason=no-client-surface), so the preferred branch can never be taken and
+ * every reconfigure -- including every drag and every dock -- demotes the promoted owned window
+ * beneath the Program Monitor's surface. The z-order mirror places it correctly and the next
+ * reconfigure undoes that, which is why the mirror measured as "runs, changes nothing".
+ *
+ * With this on, an anchor we cannot compute is simply not applied: the existing order stands and
+ * the win32 z-order mirror owns stacking, which is the Windows model. A subsurface is already
+ * above its parent by default at creation, so nothing needs the fallback to become visible.
+ */
+static BOOL neutron_no_demote(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE"); on = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return on;
+}
+
 /**********************************************************************
  *          wayland_surface_config_is_compatible
  *
@@ -1420,7 +1444,7 @@
         if (toplevel_data->client_surface &&
             toplevel_data->client_surface->toplevel_wl_surface == toplevel_surface->wl_surface)
             wl_subsurface_place_above(surface->wl_subsurface, toplevel_data->client_surface->wl_surface);
-        else
+        else if (!neutron_no_demote())
             wl_subsurface_place_above(surface->wl_subsurface, toplevel_surface->wl_surface);
         wl_surface_commit(toplevel_surface->wl_surface);
 
@@ -2507,8 +2531,8 @@
             logn++;
             for (i = 0; i < n && off < (int)sizeof(buf) - 24; i++)
                 off += snprintf(buf + off, sizeof(buf) - off, "%p%s ", applied[i], below[i] ? "(below)" : "");
-            ERR("NT-EV restack toplevel=%p t=%u order-bottom-to-top: %s\n",
-                toplevel, (unsigned)NtGetTickCount(), buf);
+            ERR("NT-EV restack toplevel=%p t=%u zlist=%u applied=%u order-bottom-to-top: %s\n",
+                toplevel, (unsigned)NtGetTickCount(), zl->count, n, buf);
         }
     }
 }
